{
  "format": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "kernelspec": {
      "name": "affon",
      "display_name": "Affon (TypeScript)",
      "language": "typescript"
    }
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell-1",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "# Illustrated NN Building Blocks\n\n`affon:nn` modules are callable blocks with parameters and mode. This notebook explains the beginner mental model for common blocks.\n\nHow to read each block:\n\n1. Ask what problem the block solves.\n2. Predict the input and output shape.\n3. Run the cell.\n4. Read which axes were preserved, transformed, normalized, or treated as time.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-2",
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "import { tensor } from 'affon:compute'\nimport nn from 'affon:nn'\n\nfunction show(label: string, value: { shape: readonly number[], to_array(): unknown }) {\n  console.log(label)\n  console.log('shape:', value.shape)\n  console.log(JSON.stringify(value.to_array()))\n}\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-3",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Linear\n\n$$\ny = xW + b\n$$\n\nFor high-rank input, leading dimensions are parallel.\n\n```txt\n[batch, time, in_features] -> Linear(in, out) -> [batch, time, out_features]\n```\n\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 190\" width=\"760\" height=\"190\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <style>.txt{font:14px sans-serif;fill:#0f172a}.mono{font:15px monospace}.p{fill:#dbeafe;stroke:#60a5fa}.o{fill:#dcfce7;stroke:#4ade80}.q{fill:#ffedd5;stroke:#fb923c}</style>\n  <text class=\"txt\" x=\"24\" y=\"28\">For every parallel position, project one feature vector.</text>\n  <rect class=\"p\" x=\"70\" y=\"70\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"mono\" x=\"91\" y=\"95\">batch</text>\n  <rect class=\"p\" x=\"155\" y=\"70\" width=\"70\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"mono\" x=\"176\" y=\"95\">time</text>\n  <rect class=\"o\" x=\"230\" y=\"70\" width=\"110\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"mono\" x=\"248\" y=\"95\">in_features</text>\n  <text class=\"mono\" x=\"375\" y=\"95\">Linear</text>\n  <rect class=\"p\" x=\"470\" y=\"70\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"mono\" x=\"491\" y=\"95\">batch</text>\n  <rect class=\"p\" x=\"555\" y=\"70\" width=\"70\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"mono\" x=\"576\" y=\"95\">time</text>\n  <rect class=\"q\" x=\"630\" y=\"70\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"mono\" x=\"648\" y=\"95\">out_features</text>\n</svg>\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-4",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "### Before You Run\n\nA Linear layer changes only the feature dimension. Predict the output shape for input `[2, 3]` through `Linear(3, 2)`.\n\n### Read The Output\n\nThe exact numbers are random because the layer is initialized randomly. The important beginner fact is the shape: each length-3 row becomes a length-2 row.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-5",
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "const linear = nn.Linear(3, 2)\nshow('linear(tensor([[1,2,3],[4,5,6]]))', linear(tensor([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])))\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-6",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Embedding\n\nAn embedding layer is a lookup table.\n\n```txt\ntoken ids: [tokens]\ntable:     [vocab, embedding_dim]\noutput:    [tokens, embedding_dim]\n```\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-7",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "### Before You Run\n\nFor token ids shaped `[2]` and an embedding dim of `3`, predict the output shape.\n\n### Read The Output\n\nEach integer id selects one row from the embedding table. The id axis stays, and a new embedding feature axis appears.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-8",
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "const embedding = nn.Embedding(5, 3)\nshow('embedding([1, 3])', embedding(tensor([1, 3], { dtype: 'i64' })))\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-9",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Sequential And Module List\n\n`Sequential` pipes one block into the next.\n\n```txt\ninput -> Linear -> activation -> Linear -> output\n```\n\n`module_list` owns repeated blocks so their parameters are included in the module state.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-10",
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "const model = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(3, 4), nn.Linear(4, 2))\nshow('Sequential output', model(tensor([[1, 2, 3]])))\nconst layers = nn.module_list([nn.Linear(3, 3), nn.Linear(3, 3)])\nconsole.log('module_list length:', layers.length)\nconsole.log('module_list parameters:', layers.parameters.length)\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-11",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Dropout\n\nDropout randomly zeros activations during training. In eval mode, it passes values through.\n\n```txt\ntrain: some values are zeroed\neval:  values pass through\n```\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-12",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "### Before You Run\n\nIn eval mode, predict whether the values will change. In train mode, some values may be randomly zeroed.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-13",
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "const dropout = nn.Dropout(0.5)\ndropout.mode('eval')\nshow('dropout eval', dropout(tensor([[1, 2], [3, 4]])))\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-14",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## LayerNorm And BatchNorm\n\nNormalization blocks rescale activations so training is easier.\n\n- `LayerNorm`: normalize within each example over the feature axis.\n- `BatchNorm`: normalize each feature using batch statistics.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-15",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "### Before You Run\n\nLayerNorm normalizes within each row over features. BatchNorm normalizes each feature using batch statistics. Predict which axis each one treats as the feature axis in the example.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-16",
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "const layerNorm = nn.LayerNorm(3)\nshow('LayerNorm over last dim', layerNorm(tensor([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])))\nconst batchNorm = nn.BatchNorm(3)\nshow('BatchNorm over features', batchNorm(tensor([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])))\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-17",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Recurrent Blocks\n\n`SimpleRNN`, `RNN`, and `LSTM` process sequences by carrying state through time.\n\n```txt\nx_t + previous hidden -> new hidden\n```\n\n`LSTM` also carries a cell state, which acts like a longer-term memory path.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-18",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "### Before You Run\n\nFor batch-first RNN input `[batch=2, time=2, input=3]` with hidden size `4`, predict the output shape.\n\n### Read The Output\n\nThe input feature axis becomes hidden size. Batch and time remain visible because the recurrent block emits a hidden vector for each time step.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-19",
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "const rnn = nn.RNN(3, 4, { batch_first: true })\nshow('RNN batch_first output: [batch=2, time=2, hidden=4]', rnn(tensor([[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], [[1, 1, 1], [2, 2, 2]]])))\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-20",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "language": "markdown",
      "source": "## Masks, Positions, Init, Diagnostics\n\n- `causal_mask` and `apply_causal_mask` prevent attention from seeing future positions.\n- `position_ids` and `sinusoidal_encoding` add position information.\n- `nn.init.*` helpers initialize tensors.\n- `nn.diagnostics.*` can check values while a model runs.\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "cell-21",
      "kind": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "source": "show('causal_mask(4)', nn.causal_mask(4))\nshow('position_ids(4)', nn.position_ids(4))\nshow('sinusoidal_encoding(4, 6)', nn.sinusoidal_encoding(4, 6))\n",
      "outputs": [],
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}
